Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.939 align:start position:0%
 
I got an email that said hi we're  developing a fully autonomous race car  would you like to be driven around the  most famous track in Britain by a  computer  sure Robo race is an organization  specializing in autonomous racing we  develop our own autonomous race cars and  we run a competition for teams to enter  to develop their own autonomous driving  software we're basically trying to  further autonomous driving technology  through competition now I'm not used to  anything like this I have never been  driven at speed around the track by a  human and I've never been driven at all  by a fully autonomous car this is the  dev bot 2 designed to take a human  passenger now the stats aren't  spectacular by supercar standards 362  horsepower from two electric motors top  speed somewhere around 135 miles now  although  azzam a civilian I'm limited to 60 and  it will take 4.1 seconds to get there  but this is only the development version  this is the version that still carries a  human unlike its sleeker cousin over  there Ronnie is robocar this is fully  autonomous there's no room for a human  whereas the dev bot gives us the  opportunity for human versus machine  type challengers it's actually got the  same powertrain on the rear axle as dev  bot it's just also got the same  powertrain again on the front axle so  it's four-wheel drive it's got twice the  power it's the car we use for our top  speed run we've actually had it up to  299 kph the sensors on the two cars are  actually the same we use a  military-grade GPS accurate up to about  two centimetres so the car knows exactly  where it is  we've got lied arse basically on the  corners of the car and on the sides to a  position where were the objects are  around the car the third is using  cameras and that's using stereo vision  to judge depth basically in the same way  as a human does with their eyes so I'm  about to go for a lap of Silverstone a  national circuit just over one and a  half miles which will take around 90  seconds from a standing start once we've  created a map of the track to say these  are the edges this is where you're  allowed to go we load that into the car  it then has algorithms to determine its  optimum racing line it then chooses what  speed it can go and when it needs to  brake when it needs to hit the throttle  based on accelerations and  knows what it can achieve from the tires  now I know because I've never been on a  track before that on that first turn my  brain is gonna think that we are coming  in too hot and it's not going to turn  and I'm just gonna need to have some  faith that the system is working and I  don't need to take over control because  I am almost certainly a worse driver  than the computer in this car  basically when you're trying to do  something for the first time we need to  be very safety conscious  we have full live telemetry so we can  see exactly what the car is doing at all  times and we have link to that what we  call a safe stop system basically button  that can turn the car off at a moment's  notice from anywhere the car can also  knows to turn itself off if it  determines it's too far off the line  it's supposed to be on and it doesn't  think it can get back there it will shut  itself down moving to drive now and then  moving out onto the track we've had a  few crashes over the last year and this  has been our first year of competition  one of the crashes that we had was  basically a hardware issue the hardware  failed and that led to the car believing  it was in a different place to where it  actually was so it acted accordingly to  go to where it thought it should be but  that was based on bad data which then  meant the car hit the wall  all right I'm lined up and I'm gonna  take my hands off the wheel and put them  here and take my feet off the pedals and  put them on the footrest to be clear we  are not going to be going over a hundred  kilometers an hour here 60 miles an hour  because there's a human in the car and  that's what we're limited to but  acceleration braking and g-forces not  limited okay ready to go all right so we  will get our side so if you're happy  we'll get ready finished happy is a  relative term but yes I am ready to go  all right copy that so ho tight we'll  launch in three two one  okay all right and I'm being assured  that this car is not just following a  pre-programmed line it knows the best  racing line to take of course  it knows the track it's been taught  where it should go but it is reacting in  real-time to attract conditions to the  fact that it's slightly slippery because  of rain earlier and the fact there is  alright the fact there is all that is a  perfect racing line the fight there is  about 70 kilos of terrified wet meat sat  in the driver's seat because that's all  I am right now it's the computer doing  everything here and it sounds like an  electric car the steering wheel is just  moving for me this is astonishing we've  actually had various event formats to  our first year of competition we've had  a race with multiple cars on track on  the same circuit like a traditional race  you've also had competitions that are  far more on a time trial basis just  trying to find different formats and see  how what's on of us racing can really  work to you the cars are identical we as  a company running all of the cars the  teams basically just provide a driver in  normal motorsport that would be a human  being in this case it's a few thousand  lines of software code so if you program  your car to go faster for a corner and  get a better exit from the corner you  will be quicker on the straight you will  be able to overtake whoo and that's  really what it's all about we really  wanted everyone to focus on the  autonomous driving rather than the car  setup thank you returning to base the I  does have information given to it on  where the other car is what the other  car is doing but at the moment that's  mainly been used for safety purposes we  can control the car from the control  room but we do that has company for  safety reasons the teams are not given  any access once the car has started its  race it's only what they've already  programmed  so that was a lot of fun that was that  was wonderful  III had a great time but I also feel  just a little bit underwhelmed because  it didn't feel like there was any risk  there and of course there's no risk  there like I'm a civilian they're gonna  limit the car they're not gonna push to  the edge but that's because there's a  person in the car and that's what  motorsport is about motorsport is about  humans pushing themselves as close to  the limit as close to the redline as  they can and without the element of risk  without that person in the car I I don't  know if it'll work as a sport Robo races  is gambling they can move the audience's  attention from people in the car to  people in the pits and like yeah that  was a lot of fun but it's a heck of a  gamble the whole business model relies  on a successful sport which relies on  people watching it so it has to be  entertaining we're kind of a strange mix  between a technology development and an  entertainment business and I think there  is actually a lot of human element still  within this although it's machines  racing it's people creating the code for  those machines something that is  forgotten in traditional motorsport in a  form of one team there's a thousand  people you just only hear about the two  that are in the car so here all the  human stories are really about those  people in the background how they've  coded the system and they're still  pushing the limits it's just arguably in  a safer manner curve isn't a human in  the car if it does go off thank you so  much to all the team at Robo race find  out more about them by pulling down the  description um  I heart still guy that was only 60 it  felt so much more than 60

